xnu 0239: what the CP did with the blit -- read the 2D state back

0236 replayed the window server's own 51-dword clearing IB and the fill
completed (`submit 1 poll 1`) while writing nothing (`nonzero 0/64`). The
replay was not faithful: Mesa writes RB_CCU_CNTL2 at the head of every
batch (measured in this GPU's stream as `4088e501 80000000`, colour cache
full at offset 0x100000), and a standalone blit without it runs with
whatever CCU geometry the last batch left. That pair now leads the replay.

Two hypotheses fit "the engine runs and memory does not change", and one
boot separates them:

  * A type-4 register write that CP_PROTECT covers is dropped with no
    error, no fault and no stall, which would leave the blit with no
    destination at all. The registers are read back after the fence
    (RB_2D_DST_INFO, RB_2D_DST, RB_2D_DST_PITCH, RB_2D_BLIT_CNTL) together
    with CP_PROTECT_STATUS and CP_HW_FAULT: if they read zero or something
    else, the CP refused the writes.
  * The client's memory is not reachable from the GPU. Every write this
    port has seen land went to a page the driver allocated, so the IB
    ends with a CP_MEM_WRITE of 0xcafe1234 into the same buffer, through
    the same IOVA: if that dword reads back, the address and the mapping
    are fine and the fault is the engine's.

FSR/GFSR are cleared before the submission and read after it, so a fault
belongs to this IB. `ccucntl=0` reproduces the earlier replay.
